// Rep Max Learn lessons: one per topic. Fields: s, t, kind (method, idea or writing), why, hook (idea topics only), chunks, example (task, steps, answer), selfx, faded, trap, recap. Written by Sonnet, checked by Opus.
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.1.3.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Osmosis questions on Paper 1 often use potato cylinders from required practical 3, so you need to explain which way water moves and why mass changes.", "hook": {"q": "A shrivelled raisin is left in a glass of pure water for several hours. What happens to it?", "o": ["It shrinks, because water moves out of the raisin into the pure water", "It stays the same size, because water moves in and out equally", "It swells, because water moves into the raisin from the pure water", "It swells, because the sugar inside moves out into the water"], "a": 2, "f": ["Not quite. The raisin contains dissolved sugar, so it is more concentrated than pure water. Water moves towards the more concentrated side, which is into the raisin.", "Not quite. Water would only move in and out equally if the two sides had the same concentration. Pure water is far more dilute than the inside of a raisin.", "Right. The raisin's contents are more concentrated than pure water, so water moves into the raisin and it swells. This is osmosis.", "The outcome is right but the reason is not. It is water that moves, not sugar, and the water moves into the raisin."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What osmosis is", "b": "**Osmosis** is the movement of water from a **dilute** solution to a more concentrated solution, through a **partially permeable membrane**. The membrane lets small water molecules through but blocks larger solute molecules. Water passes both ways, but more goes from the dilute side. So the net movement is towards the concentrated side."}, {"h": "Effect on plant tissue", "b": "Potato cells have a partially permeable membrane. Compare the outside solution with the cell contents. If the outside is **more dilute**, water enters and the mass increases. If it is **more concentrated**, water leaves and the mass decreases. If both are equal, there is **no net movement** and no change in mass."}, {"h": "Required practical 3", "b": "Cut potato cylinders of equal size. Put each in a different sucrose concentration, including pure water. Blot dry and weigh each cylinder before, and again after the same time. Calculate **percentage change in mass** = change ÷ starting mass × 100. Keep **control variables** the same: volume, temperature, time and cylinder size."}, {"h": "Finding the zero point", "b": "Plot percentage change (y) against concentration (x). Where the line **crosses 0%**, there is no net water movement. The solution then has the **same concentration** as the cells. Read where it crosses, not the midpoint. Example: +3% at 0.2 and −2% at 0.4 mol/dm³ crosses at 0.32 mol/dm³."}], "example": {"task": "A potato cylinder is left in pure water for 30 minutes. Its mass increases. Explain why.", "steps": [{"do": "Pure water is more dilute than the liquid inside the potato cells.", "why": "Direction depends on comparing concentrations, so you state this first."}, {"do": "The cell membrane is partially permeable, so water can pass through but dissolved solutes cannot.", "why": "Osmosis only happens across a partially permeable membrane."}, {"do": "Water moves into the cells by osmosis, from the dilute solution to the more concentrated one.", "why": "This is the direction rule for osmosis."}, {"do": "More water enters than leaves, so the cells gain water and the cylinder's mass increases.", "why": "It links the water movement to the mass change the question asks about."}], "answer": "Pure water is more dilute than the potato cell contents. The cell membrane is partially permeable, so water can pass but solutes cannot. Water moves into the cells by osmosis, from dilute to concentrated. There is a net gain of water, so the mass increases."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer begin by comparing the water with the cell contents?", "o": ["Because osmosis needs energy, which depends on how concentrated the cell contents are", "Because water moves towards the more concentrated solution, so this sets the direction", "Because sugar moves out of the cell until both concentrations are the same", "Because the cell contents are always more dilute than any solution outside"], "a": 1, "f": ["Osmosis is passive and needs no energy. Mixing it up with active transport is the slip here.", "Right. Water moves from the more dilute solution to the more concentrated one. You must compare the two before you can say which way it goes.", "Solutes cannot cross a partially permeable membrane. In osmosis only water moves, so this describes the wrong process.", "Cell contents can be more dilute or more concentrated than the outside. Only pure water is certain to be more dilute than them."]}, "faded": {"task": "A potato cylinder is left in a concentrated sucrose solution. Its mass decreases. Explain why.", "given": ["The sucrose solution is more concentrated than the liquid inside the potato cells.", "The cell membrane is partially permeable, so water can pass through but sucrose cannot."], "q": "What is the next point in the answer?", "o": ["Sucrose moves out of the cells to balance the concentrations, so the mass falls.", "Water moves from the concentrated solution into the cells, so the mass falls.", "Water is pulled out of the cells by active transport, so the mass falls.", "Water moves out of the cells into the solution by osmosis, so the mass falls."], "a": 3, "f": ["Solutes do not cross a partially permeable membrane, as the last step said. It is water that moves in osmosis.", "The direction is reversed. Water moves towards the more concentrated solution, which is outside the cells. Water entering the cells would also raise the mass.", "Osmosis is passive, so it does not use active transport. Name osmosis and give the direction of water movement.", "Right. The cells are more dilute than the solution, so water moves out by osmosis. There is a net loss of water, so the mass falls."]}, "trap": "Writing that sugar or other solutes move, or that water moves from concentrated to dilute. Only water crosses the membrane, and it moves from the dilute solution to the concentrated one. Check which side is more dilute first.", "recap": "Water moves by osmosis from the more dilute solution to the more concentrated one, through a partially permeable membrane."}
{"s": "biology", "t": "4.2.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Heart and blood vessel questions appear in Paper 1 and often ask you to name vessels, trace blood flow or link structure to function.", "hook": {"q": "Blood goes from the heart to the lungs, then back to the heart before going round the body. What is the most likely reason?", "o": ["The heart filters the blood after the lungs have added oxygen to it", "Blood can only reach one organ at a time, so it has to take turns", "Blood loses pressure in the narrow lung vessels, so the heart pumps it again", "The lungs make new blood cells that the heart must then collect"], "a": 2, "f": ["Filtering is not the heart's job. The heart is a pump, and the lungs add oxygen by gas exchange.", "Blood flows to many organs at once. The real reason for the second pump is pressure, not turn-taking.", "Right. Lung capillaries are very narrow, so pressure drops. The heart pumps the blood again to push it round the body.", "New blood cells are made in bone marrow, not the lungs. The lungs exchange gases with the blood."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Double circulation", "b": "Blood passes through the heart twice on each full trip. This is a **double circulatory system**. One loop goes from the heart to the **lungs** and back, so blood picks up oxygen. The other loop goes from the heart to the **body** and back."}, {"h": "Chambers and valves", "b": "The heart has four chambers. The two **atria** (upper) receive blood. The two **ventricles** (lower) pump blood out. **Valves** stop blood flowing backwards. The left ventricle has a thicker wall than the right, because it pumps blood at higher pressure all round the body."}, {"h": "Route through the heart", "b": "Deoxygenated blood: vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → **pulmonary artery** → lungs. Oxygenated blood: **pulmonary vein** → left atrium → left ventricle → **aorta** → body. The right side serves the lungs. The left side serves the body."}, {"h": "Three types of vessel", "b": "**Arteries** carry blood away from the heart, at high pressure. Their walls are thick, with muscle and elastic fibres, and the lumen is narrow. **Veins** carry blood towards the heart, at low pressure. They have thinner walls, a wider lumen and valves. **Capillaries** have walls one cell thick, so substances diffuse quickly."}], "example": {"task": "Describe how oxygenated blood travels from the lungs to the rest of the body. Name the vessels and the heart chambers it passes through. (5 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Blood leaves the lungs in the pulmonary vein.", "why": "This vessel carries oxygenated blood towards the heart, so it is a vein."}, {"do": "The blood enters the left atrium.", "why": "The pulmonary vein joins the left side of the heart, and atria receive blood."}, {"do": "It passes through a valve into the left ventricle.", "why": "The valve keeps blood moving one way. Ventricles are the chambers that pump."}, {"do": "The left ventricle contracts and pumps the blood at high pressure into the aorta.", "why": "The thick wall gives high pressure. The aorta is the artery that leaves the left side."}, {"do": "The aorta and its branches carry the blood to the organs, where capillaries let oxygen diffuse out.", "why": "Arteries take blood away from the heart. Capillary walls are one cell thick."}], "answer": "Oxygenated blood flows from the lungs along the pulmonary vein to the left atrium. It passes through a valve into the left ventricle. The ventricle contracts and pumps it at high pressure into the aorta, which carries it in arteries to the body's organs."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta, not the pulmonary artery?", "o": ["The pulmonary artery only carries blood back towards the heart", "The aorta leads to the body; the pulmonary artery leads to the lungs", "The aorta is a vein, so it is the vessel that can carry oxygenated blood", "The right ventricle is the only chamber that can pump blood into an artery"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Arteries always carry blood away from the heart. The pulmonary artery carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs.", "Right. The left ventricle serves the body through the aorta. The right ventricle serves the lungs through the pulmonary artery.", "Wrong. The aorta is an artery, since it carries blood away from the heart. Oxygen content does not decide the vessel type.", "Wrong. Both ventricles pump blood into arteries: the left into the aorta, the right into the pulmonary artery."]}, "faded": {"task": "Describe how deoxygenated blood travels from the body to the lungs. Name the vessels and chambers it passes through.", "given": ["Blood returns from the body in the vena cava.", "It enters the right atrium."], "q": "What is the next step in the answer?", "o": ["It flows straight into the pulmonary artery, which takes it to the lungs.", "It passes through a valve into the left ventricle.", "It enters the pulmonary vein, which takes it to the lungs.", "It passes through a valve into the right ventricle, which pumps it out."], "a": 3, "f": ["This skips a chamber. Blood must go through the right ventricle before it can enter the pulmonary artery.", "The left side holds oxygenated blood. Deoxygenated blood stays on the right side until it reaches the lungs.", "The pulmonary vein carries blood from the lungs to the heart. Blood going to the lungs travels in the pulmonary artery.", "Right. The right atrium passes blood through a valve to the right ventricle. The ventricle then pumps it into the pulmonary artery."]}, "trap": "Do not say veins always carry deoxygenated blood. Use direction instead: arteries carry blood away from the heart, veins towards it. The pulmonary artery is deoxygenated and the pulmonary vein is oxygenated.", "recap": "Arteries carry blood away from the heart, veins towards it; the right side pumps to the lungs, the left to the body."}
{"s": "business", "t": "1.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exam questions often give you a business and ask which type of market research it should use and why, so you need to know what each type gives and costs.", "hook": {"q": "A new bakery wants to know roughly how much bread people in its town buy. What is the quickest, cheapest way to start?", "o": ["Stop shoppers outside and interview each one", "Look up existing local population and spending figures", "Hold a focus group with eight local families", "Watch customers at a rival bakery all week"], "a": 1, "f": ["Slow and costly, and it only covers the people who happen to pass by.", "Right. The figures already exist, so nothing needs collecting. Using data someone else gathered is secondary research.", "A focus group takes time to set up and gives opinions from a few people, not town-wide totals.", "Watching takes a week and only shows one rival's customers, not the whole town."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Primary research", "b": "**Primary research** is new data you collect yourself, for your own question. Methods include surveys, questionnaires, **focus groups** and **observation**. It is up to date and fits your product exactly. But it takes time and money, and a small sample can be biased."}, {"h": "Secondary research", "b": "**Secondary research** uses data that already exists, collected by someone else. Examples are **market reports**, government statistics and websites. It is quick and cheap, and shows the wider market, such as its size. But it may be out of date or not answer your exact question."}, {"h": "Using both together", "b": "Many firms start with **secondary research** for cheap background. Then they use **primary research** to fill gaps about their own product. Data is more **reliable** if the sample is large and random, and comes from the target market."}], "example": {"task": "Aisha plans to start a business selling handmade dog treats. Explain how she could use both secondary and primary research.", "steps": [{"do": "Aisha could first read an online market report on UK spending on pet food. This is secondary research, because someone else collected the data.", "why": "Names the type correctly and links it to her market."}, {"do": "The report is cheap and quick. It shows how big the market is and whether it is growing, so she can see if the market is worth entering.", "why": "Gives a benefit of secondary research, applied to her business."}, {"do": "However, the report cannot tell her what local dog owners think of her recipes or what price they would pay.", "why": "Shows the gap, which is why primary research is needed."}, {"do": "So she could give a questionnaire to a random sample of dog owners at a local park. This is primary research, as she collects new data first-hand.", "why": "Names primary research and a method, aimed at her target market."}, {"do": "Together, the report shows the market is worth entering and the questionnaire shows what to sell and at what price. This lowers her risk of a poor decision.", "why": "Links both types to a decision, which earns the final mark."}], "answer": "Aisha could use a market report (secondary) to find the size of the pet food market cheaply. She could then use a questionnaire (primary) with local dog owners to test her recipes and price. Together they reduce risk."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Aisha use the market report before the questionnaire?", "o": ["Secondary data is always more reliable than primary data", "Market reports count as primary data because she bought them", "It cheaply shows if the market is worth entering before she pays for primary", "The exam board says secondary research must always be done first"], "a": 2, "f": ["Reliability depends on the sample and the source, not the type. Secondary data can be out of date.", "A report is secondary however it is obtained. Someone else collected the data first.", "Right. Cheap background first shows whether the market is worth entering. Then she spends time and money only on the gaps.", "There is no such rule. Starting with secondary research is a sensible choice, not a requirement."]}, "faded": {"task": "A new gym wants to know which fitness classes people in its town would use. It has the figures below. What should it do next?", "given": ["A government report shows 12,000 adults live in the town. This is secondary research.", "It is cheap, but it does not show which classes people would use."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Use a report on classes at gyms in another city", "Ask the eight gym staff which classes they like", "Read more government population statistics", "Survey a random sample of local adults on class choices"], "a": 3, "f": ["Still secondary, and about another city. It does not show what people in this town want.", "Staff are a tiny, biased group. They are not the target market.", "More population figures are secondary data and still will not show class preferences.", "Right. New first-hand data from a random sample of local people fills the gap the report leaves."]}, "trap": "Do not call data primary just because you found it yourself online. Primary means you collected it first-hand for your own question. If someone else collected it first, it is secondary.", "recap": "Primary data is collected by you, first-hand; secondary data already exists. Secondary gives cheap background, primary answers your own questions."}
{"s": "business", "t": "1.3.2", "kind": "method", "why": "Paper 1 often has a break-even or profit calculation, worth several marks, and you may be asked to read a break-even diagram.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Revenue, costs and profit", "b": "**Revenue** is the money from sales: $\\text{price} \\times \\text{quantity sold}$. **Fixed costs** stay the same whatever you sell, such as rent. **Variable costs** rise with output: cost per unit $\\times$ units. Total costs $=$ fixed $+$ variable. Profit $=$ total revenue $-$ total costs."}, {"h": "Interest on loans", "b": "**Interest** is the extra you pay back when you borrow. You find it as a percentage: $\\frac{\\text{total repayment} - \\text{amount borrowed}}{\\text{amount borrowed}} \\times 100$. If you borrow £10,000 and repay £10,500, that is $\\frac{500}{10{,}000} \\times 100 = 5\\%$. Interest is an extra **cost**, so it lowers profit."}, {"h": "Break-even and margin of safety", "b": "**Break-even** is the output where total revenue equals total costs: no profit, no loss. Each sale leaves price $-$ variable cost per unit to pay off fixed costs. So break-even $=$ fixed costs $\\div$ that amount. **Margin of safety** $=$ actual output $-$ break-even output."}, {"h": "Reading a break-even diagram", "b": "Output is along the bottom, £ up the side. The revenue line starts at zero. The **total cost** line starts at the fixed-cost level. They cross at the **break-even point**. To the right, revenue is above costs: profit. To the left: loss. Read the margin of safety along the output axis."}], "example": {"task": "A firm makes phone cases. Fixed costs are £9,000 a month. Each case sells for £12 and has variable costs of £4. It sells 1,500 a month. Find break-even output, margin of safety and profit.", "steps": [{"do": "Price minus variable cost per unit: £12 − £4 = £8.", "why": "This is what each case sells for after its own variable cost, so it is what goes towards fixed costs."}, {"do": "Break-even = fixed costs ÷ £8 = 9,000 ÷ 8 = 1,125 cases.", "why": "This is how many £8 amounts are needed to cover £9,000 of fixed costs."}, {"do": "Margin of safety = actual output − break-even = 1,500 − 1,125 = 375 cases.", "why": "It shows how far sales could fall before the firm makes a loss."}, {"do": "Revenue = £12 × 1,500 = £18,000. Variable costs = £4 × 1,500 = £6,000. Total costs = £9,000 + £6,000 = £15,000.", "why": "Profit needs both total revenue and total costs, and total costs include fixed and variable."}, {"do": "Profit = £18,000 − £15,000 = £3,000.", "why": "Check: 375 cases above break-even × £8 = £3,000. Both methods agree."}], "answer": "Break-even is 1,125 cases a month. The margin of safety is 375 cases. Profit is £3,000 a month."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 1 take the £4 variable cost off the £12 price before dividing?", "o": ["Because variable costs are paid only once, at the start of the month", "Because the £12 price is too large a number to divide into fixed costs", "Because each case sold covers its own £4 cost, leaving £8 towards fixed costs", "Because profit is always found by taking variable costs off the price"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Variable costs are paid on every unit made. It is fixed costs that stay the same whatever the output.", "Wrong. The size of the price is not the issue. Using the full price ignores that each unit has its own cost to cover.", "Right. Only the £8 left after variable costs can pay off fixed costs, so that is the amount you divide by.", "Wrong. This confuses profit with the break-even step. Profit is revenue minus total costs, and fixed costs are included."]}, "faded": {"task": "A firm has fixed costs of £4,800 a month. Each unit sells for £9 and has variable costs of £5. It makes 1,500 units a month. Find the margin of safety.", "given": ["Price − variable cost per unit = £9 − £5 = £4", "Break-even = 4,800 ÷ 4 = 1,200 units"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Fixed costs ÷ actual output: 4,800 ÷ 1,500 = 3.2 units", "Actual output − break-even: 1,500 − 1,200 = 300 units", "Break-even − actual output: 1,200 − 1,500 = −300 units", "Actual output × £4: 1,500 × £4 = £6,000"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. This divides the wrong way round and gives a cost per unit, not a number of units.", "Right. Margin of safety is actual output minus break-even output, so 300 units.", "Wrong. The subtraction is the wrong way round. A negative margin of safety would mean a loss, but 1,500 is above break-even.", "Wrong. This is the total left after variable costs. It is not the margin of safety, which is a gap in units."]}, "trap": "Dividing fixed costs by the selling price instead of by price minus variable cost per unit. Work out the amount left per unit first, then divide. Margin of safety is usually in units.", "recap": "Break-even = fixed costs ÷ (price − variable cost per unit); margin of safety = actual output − break-even output."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.1.1.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Electronic structure explains the periodic table, reactivity and bonding later in the course, and short questions on it appear in Paper 1.", "hook": {"q": "A carbon atom has 6 electrons. The first shell, nearest the nucleus, can hold only 2. Where do the other 4 most likely go?", "o": ["Into the first shell, because a shell can hold any number", "Into the next shell out, the second shell", "Into a shell far from the nucleus, to keep them apart", "Nowhere, because only 2 electrons can be in an atom"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The first shell is full at 2 electrons, so any extra electrons cannot fit there.", "Right. When the first shell is full, electrons go into the next shell out. This idea of filling from the inside is how all electronic structures are built.", "Wrong. Electrons do not skip shells. They fill the lowest energy level first, then the next one out.", "Wrong. An atom can have many more than 2 electrons. They are spread over several shells."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Electrons sit in shells", "b": "Electrons move around the nucleus in **shells**, also called **energy levels**. The first shell is closest to the nucleus and has the lowest energy. Electrons always go into the **lowest available energy level** first."}, {"h": "How many each shell holds", "b": "The **first shell** holds up to 2 electrons. The **second shell** holds up to 8. For the first 20 elements, the third also holds up to 8. A new shell only starts once the shell inside it is **full**."}, {"h": "Writing the structure", "b": "Write the number in each shell as a list, from the inside outwards, with commas between. In a neutral atom, the **number of electrons** equals the **atomic number**. The numbers in your list must add up to it."}, {"h": "What the numbers tell you", "b": "The number of electrons in the **outer shell** matches the **group number** for Groups 1 to 7. The number of occupied shells matches the **period number**. So 2,8,3 is in Group 3, Period 3."}], "example": {"task": "Chlorine has atomic number 17. Explain how to find its electronic structure and use it to say which group and period chlorine is in.", "steps": [{"do": "Atomic number 17 means 17 protons. A neutral atom has 17 electrons.", "why": "Neutral atoms have equal numbers of protons and electrons, so this is the total to place."}, {"do": "Put 2 electrons in the first shell. That leaves 15.", "why": "The lowest energy level fills first, and it holds a maximum of 2."}, {"do": "Put 8 electrons in the second shell. That leaves 7.", "why": "The second shell holds 8 and must be full before the third shell starts."}, {"do": "Put the remaining 7 in the third shell. The structure is 2,8,7. Check: 2 + 8 + 7 = 17.", "why": "7 is fewer than 8, so the third shell is not full. The total check shows nothing is missing."}, {"do": "The outer shell has 7 electrons, so chlorine is in Group 7. Three shells are used, so it is in Period 3.", "why": "Outer electrons give the group. Occupied shells give the period."}], "answer": "Chlorine has 17 electrons. They fill from the inside: 2 in the first shell, 8 in the second, 7 in the third, giving 2,8,7. The outer shell has 7 electrons, so it is in Group 7. It has three shells, so it is in Period 3."}, "selfx": {"q": "In the chlorine example, why are 8 electrons put in the second shell before any go in the third?", "o": ["Every shell in every atom must contain exactly 8 electrons", "The third shell is nearest the nucleus, so it has to fill last", "The second shell has lower energy than the third and holds 8, so it fills first", "The group number tells you how many electrons go in the second shell"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Only the second and third shells hold 8 here. The first holds 2, and outer shells can have fewer than 8 electrons.", "Wrong. The first shell is nearest the nucleus, then the second, then the third. The nearest shells fill first, not last.", "Right. Electrons fill the lowest energy level first. The second shell is lower than the third, and it holds 8, so it must be full first.", "Wrong. The group number matches the outer shell electrons, not the second shell. It is a result of the structure, not a rule for filling."]}, "faded": {"task": "Calcium has atomic number 20. Work out its electronic structure.", "given": ["A neutral calcium atom has 20 electrons.", "Shell 1 takes 2 electrons, leaving 18.", "Shell 2 takes 8 electrons, leaving 10."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Put all 10 in the third shell, giving 2,8,10", "Put 8 in the third shell, then the last 2 in a fourth shell: 2,8,8,2", "Put 2 in the third shell and 8 in the fourth shell: 2,8,2,8", "Put 8 in the third shell and stop, giving 2,8,8"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. For the first 20 elements the third shell holds only 8. Putting 10 in it breaks the rule.", "Right. The third shell holds 8, leaving 2 for the fourth shell. This gives 2,8,8,2, which adds up to 20.", "Wrong. This fills the fourth shell before the third is full. Each shell must be full before the next one starts.", "Wrong. 2 + 8 + 8 = 18, not 20. Two electrons are missing, so check that your total matches the atomic number."]}, "trap": "Starting a new shell before the one inside is full, or not checking that the numbers add up to the atomic number. Fill 2, then 8, then 8, then add your numbers.", "recap": "Fill shells from the inside (2, 8, 8), write the numbers in order, and check they add up to the atomic number."}
{"s": "chemistry", "t": "4.3.1.2", "kind": "method", "why": "You need relative formula mass in almost every calculation in this unit, including moles, and it often appears as a short calculation question on its own.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Adding up the Mr", "b": "The **relative formula mass (Mr)** of a compound is the sum of the **relative atomic masses (Ar)** of all the atoms in its formula. Take each Ar from the periodic table or the question. Multiply by the number of atoms of that element, then add."}, {"h": "Brackets in formulae", "b": "A small number (a **subscript**) just after a **bracket** multiplies everything inside it. In Mg(OH)₂ there are 2 O atoms and 2 H atoms. Work out the bracket first, then multiply. Missing the number after a bracket is a common way to lose marks."}, {"h": "Percentage by mass", "b": "The **percentage by mass** of an element tells you how much of the compound's mass is that element. Use $\\frac{A_r \\times \\text{number of atoms}}{M_r \\text{ of compound}} \\times 100$. Find the **Mr of the compound** first. Count every atom of the element, not just one."}, {"h": "Check your answer", "b": "The percentages of all the elements in a compound add up to **100%**. Your answer for one element must be between 0 and 100. Round only at the end, and give the number of **significant figures** the question asks for."}], "example": {"task": "Calculate the percentage by mass of oxygen in calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂. Ar: Ca = 40, O = 16, H = 1. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.", "steps": [{"do": "Count the atoms: 1 Ca, 2 O, 2 H.", "why": "The 2 after the bracket multiplies both O and H inside it."}, {"do": "Mr = 40 + (2 × 16) + (2 × 1) = 40 + 32 + 2 = 74.", "why": "Mr is the sum of the Ar of every atom in the formula."}, {"do": "Mass of oxygen in one formula unit = 2 × 16 = 32.", "why": "There are two O atoms, so both count, not just one."}, {"do": "Percentage = 32 ÷ 74 × 100 = 43.243...%", "why": "This is the oxygen's share of the whole Mr, written as a percentage."}, {"do": "Round to 3 significant figures: 43.2%.", "why": "The question asks for 3 significant figures, so round only now."}], "answer": "The percentage of oxygen in Ca(OH)₂ is 43.2% by mass."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the mass of oxygen divided by the Mr of the whole compound in step 4?", "o": ["Because the Mr is always larger than 100, so the answer comes out as a percentage", "Because the percentage is oxygen's share of the total mass of the compound", "Because the Ar of calcium and hydrogen are not needed in the calculation", "Because dividing by the Mr removes the units from the Ar of oxygen"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The Mr of a compound can be any value, and here it is 74. You divide by it to compare with the whole, not to reach 100.", "Right. A percentage compares a part with the whole. The part is the mass of oxygen and the whole is the Mr of the compound.", "Wrong. Ca and H are needed, because they make up the Mr that you divide by. Leaving them out would give the wrong total.", "Wrong. Ar values have no units. You divide by Mr to find the fraction of the mass that is oxygen."]}, "faded": {"task": "Calculate the percentage by mass of oxygen in magnesium nitrate, Mg(NO₃)₂. Ar: Mg = 24, N = 14, O = 16.", "given": ["Ar values: Mg = 24, N = 14, O = 16.", "Mr = 24 + 2 × (14 + 48) = 24 + 124 = 148."], "q": "What is the mass of oxygen to put on the top of the fraction?", "o": ["3 × 16 = 48", "2 × 16 = 32", "6 × 16 = 96", "1 × 16 = 16"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This counts only the 3 O atoms in one NO₃ group. The 2 outside the bracket doubles this to 6 O atoms.", "Wrong. This uses the 2 outside the bracket but ignores the 3 in NO₃. Each nitrate group has 3 O atoms.", "Right. Each NO₃ has 3 O atoms and there are 2 NO₃ groups, so 6 O atoms. 6 × 16 = 96. Then 96 ÷ 148 × 100 = 64.9%.", "Wrong. This uses the Ar of just one oxygen atom. You must multiply by the number of O atoms in the formula."]}, "trap": "Using the Ar of just one atom, or ignoring the number outside a bracket. Count every atom of the element, and multiply anything inside a bracket by the number after it.", "recap": "Find the Mr by adding every atom, then divide (Ar × number of atoms) by the Mr and multiply by 100."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.1.5", "kind": "method", "why": "The exam often asks for the speed, direction or lever order of a mechanism, and gear and pulley calculations are quick marks if you follow the same method each time.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Types of movement", "b": "**Rotary** movement goes round in a circle. Linear goes one way in a straight line. **Reciprocating** goes back and forth in a straight line. **Oscillating** swings back and forth along an arc. A cam turns rotary movement into reciprocating movement. A bell crank linkage turns movement through 90°."}, {"h": "Gears: direction and ratio", "b": "Meshing gears turn opposite ways. An **idler** gear between driver and output makes them turn the same way. **Gear ratio** = driven teeth ÷ driver teeth. Speed out = speed in ÷ ratio. A larger driven gear turns slower but gives more turning force."}, {"h": "Pulleys and belts", "b": "Pulleys use the same ratio as gears, but with **diameters** instead of teeth: ratio = driven diameter ÷ driver diameter. An **open belt** keeps the direction the same. A **crossed belt** reverses it. A larger driven pulley turns slower."}, {"h": "Levers: three orders", "b": "Every lever has an effort, a load and a pivot. In a **first order** lever the pivot is in the middle, like a seesaw. In a **second order** lever the load is in the middle, like a wheelbarrow. In a **third order** lever the effort is in the middle, like tweezers."}], "example": {"task": "Gear A (driver, 20 teeth) turns clockwise at 240 rpm. It meshes with idler B (40 teeth), which meshes with gear C (output, 60 teeth). Find the speed and direction of C.", "steps": [{"do": "Find the direction. A is clockwise, so B is anticlockwise, so C is clockwise.", "why": "Each meshing pair turns opposite ways. Two reversals bring C back to the same direction as A."}, {"do": "Ignore the idler. Use the driver (20 teeth) and the output (60 teeth) only.", "why": "The idler only changes direction. Only the first and last gears set the speed."}, {"do": "Ratio = driven ÷ driver = 60 ÷ 20 = 3, so the ratio is 3:1.", "why": "This is the gear ratio formula, with the output gear as the driven gear."}, {"do": "Speed out = 240 ÷ 3 = 80 rpm.", "why": "Speed out = speed in ÷ ratio."}, {"do": "Check: C is bigger than A, so it should turn slower. 80 rpm is less than 240 rpm.", "why": "A quick sense check catches an upside-down ratio."}], "answer": "Gear C turns at 80 rpm clockwise."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked example leave out the idler's 40 teeth when it works out the ratio?", "o": ["The idler does not turn, so it cannot change the speed", "The idler's effect cancels out, so only the first and last gears set the speed", "The idler has the most teeth, so it is the largest gear", "Idlers are never counted because they are always the middle gear"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. The idler does turn. Its teeth drive C and are driven by A, so its effect cancels out, and this is why it only changes direction.", "Right. The idler is driven by A and then drives C, so its tooth count cancels out. It changes direction only.", "Wrong. Having the most teeth is not the reason. Even a tiny idler would be left out, because only the driver and output set the speed.", "Wrong. Being in the middle is not the reason. The idler is left out because its effect on speed cancels out, not because of where it sits."], "a_note": null}, "faded": {"task": "Driver gear P (48 teeth) turns anticlockwise at 100 rpm. Idler Q has 16 teeth. Output gear R has 12 teeth. Find the speed and direction of R.", "given": ["Direction: P anticlockwise, Q clockwise, so R is anticlockwise.", "Ignore the idler. Use driver 48 teeth and output 12 teeth."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Ratio = 48 ÷ 12 = 4, so speed out = 100 ÷ 4 = 25 rpm", "Ratio = 16 ÷ 48 = 1/3, so speed out = 100 ÷ (1/3) = 300 rpm", "Ratio = 12 ÷ 48 = 0.25, so speed out = 100 ÷ 0.25 = 400 rpm", "Ratio = 12 ÷ 48 = 0.25, so speed out = 100 × 0.25 = 25 rpm"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. This turns the ratio upside down. The ratio is driven ÷ driver, which is 12 ÷ 48. Here the small output gear should turn faster, not slower.", "Wrong. This puts the idler's 16 teeth into the ratio. Only the driver (48) and the output (12) are used.", "Right. The ratio is 12 ÷ 48 = 0.25. Dividing 100 rpm by 0.25 gives 400 rpm. The small output gear turns faster.", "Wrong. The ratio is correct, but speed out = speed in ÷ ratio. Multiplying makes the small output gear turn slower, which fails the sense check."]}, "trap": "Putting the idler's teeth into the ratio. Only the driver and the output gear set the speed. The idler only changes direction. Cross out the idler before you calculate.", "recap": "Ratio = driven ÷ driver, speed out = speed in ÷ ratio, and the idler changes direction only."}
{"s": "dt", "t": "3.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "The written paper often shows a product and asks you to name the forces on it, then say how to stiffen it. This lesson covers both.", "hook": {"q": "You clamp a plastic ruler flat on a desk at one end, sticking out over the edge, and press down on the free end. Which surface is stretched?", "o": ["The bottom surface, because it is nearest the desk", "Neither surface. The ruler only gets shorter overall", "The top surface, on the outside of the curve", "Both surfaces are stretched by the same amount"], "a": 2, "f": ["Nearest the desk is not what matters. The bottom is on the inside of the curve, so it is squashed, not stretched.", "The ruler does not simply shorten. Bending stretches one surface and squashes the other.", "The top is on the outside of the curve, so it has further to go and is stretched. This is tension.", "Bending does not treat both sides alike. One side is stretched and the other is squashed."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Tension and compression", "b": "**Tension** is a pulling force that tries to stretch a material, like the cable holding up a swing. **Compression** is a pushing force that tries to squash it, like the legs of a stool when you sit on it. Tension pulls apart. Compression pushes together."}, {"h": "Bending", "b": "A **bending** force makes a beam curve under a load. On a **cantilever**, which is fixed at one end only, a load pushes the free end down. The top surface is stretched, so it is in tension. The bottom surface is squashed, so it is in compression."}, {"h": "Torsion and shear", "b": "**Torsion** is a twisting force along the length of an object, like turning a screwdriver or wringing out a cloth. **Shear** is when two forces act in opposite directions very close together and try to slide one part past another, like scissors cutting paper or a bolt in a hinge."}, {"h": "Stiffening and reinforcing", "b": "**Triangulation** uses diagonal braces, because a triangle cannot change shape without its sides changing length. **Ribs** add depth along a thin part. Folding or corrugating sheet material does the same. **Laminating** bonds thin layers together to make a stiffer, stronger board."}], "example": {"task": "A wooden shelf is fixed to a wall at one end only. A heavy box sits on its free end. Explain the forces in the shelf and how to stiffen it.", "steps": [{"do": "The box pushes the free end down, so the shelf acts as a cantilever and is under a bending force.", "why": "Name the overall force first. A load on a beam fixed at one end causes bending."}, {"do": "The top surface is on the outside of the curve. It is stretched, so it is in tension.", "why": "The outside of a curve gets longer. Stretching means tension."}, {"do": "The bottom surface is on the inside of the curve. It is squashed, so it is in compression.", "why": "The inside of a curve gets shorter. Squashing means compression."}, {"do": "To stiffen it, fix a triangular bracket under the shelf, from the wall to the shelf, so the angle cannot change.", "why": "Triangulation stops the shelf bending down. The answer links the method to the problem."}], "answer": "The box makes the shelf bend like a cantilever. The top is in tension and the bottom is in compression. A triangular bracket under the shelf would stiffen it by triangulation, stopping the shelf bending down."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the top surface of the shelf is in tension?", "o": ["Tension is the name for any force that pushes down from above", "Bending puts the top on the outside of the curve, so it gets longer and is stretched", "The wall holds the shelf, so the top is pulled towards the wall", "The box twists the shelf, and twisting always puts the top in tension"], "a": 1, "f": ["Tension is a pulling force that stretches, not a downward push. The downward load causes the bending, but the stretch is what makes it tension.", "The outside of a curve is longer, so the top is stretched. Stretching is tension.", "The wall holds the shelf in place. It is not what stretches the top surface. The curve of the bend does that.", "A load on the end of a shelf causes bending, not torsion. Torsion is twisting along the length."]}, "faded": {"task": "A flat steel strip is clamped at one end. A weight hangs from its free end. Explain the forces in the strip and how to stiffen it.", "given": ["The weight makes the strip bend downwards, so it acts as a cantilever.", "The top surface is stretched, so it is in tension.", "The bottom surface is squashed, so it is in compression."], "q": "Which point completes the answer by saying how to stiffen the strip?", "o": ["Make the strip thinner so that it carries less of the load", "Paint the strip, because a coating stops the force reaching the metal", "Drill a row of holes along it, because a lighter strip bends less", "Fold the long edges down into a channel, adding depth to resist bending"], "a": 3, "f": ["A thinner strip is easier to bend. The load is the same, and less material means less stiffness.", "Paint protects against rust but does not change how stiff the strip is. Forces act through a coating.", "Holes remove material, so the strip is less stiff and bends more. Its own weight is tiny compared with the hanging load.", "Folding the edges adds depth to a flat strip. A deeper shape resists bending far better than a flat one."]}, "trap": "Swapping tension and compression on a bent beam, or calling every twist 'bending'. Picture the load pushing the free end down: the top stretches, the bottom squashes. Twisting along the length is torsion.", "recap": "A loaded cantilever has tension on top and compression underneath; torsion twists, shear slides, and triangles, ribs, folds and laminating stiffen."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p1-q2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Q2 is worth 8 marks on Paper 1, and most of them come from explaining the effects of the writer's specific word choices.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Quote, name, explain", "b": "Make a **point** about what the writer creates, such as danger or sadness. Give a short **quotation** of a few words. Then explain what those words suggest. Use **subject terminology**, such as 'verb' or 'personification', where it helps, but naming a technique earns little on its own."}, {"h": "Zoom in on one word", "b": "Choose the most powerful word, often a verb or adjective. Ask what it suggests: its **connotations**. 'Slithered' links to snakes, so a person seems sly. This close look at a **word choice** is where higher marks are earned."}, {"h": "Finish with the effect", "b": "End each point with the **effect on the reader**. What do they picture or feel? Use phrases such as 'this suggests' and 'this makes the reader'. Link it to what the writer is creating, such as **tension** or sympathy."}], "example": {"task": "Read: \"The wind clawed at her coat. She stumbled on, the dark lane swallowing her footsteps.\" How does the writer use language to create a threatening setting?", "steps": [{"do": "The writer presents the wind as hostile through personification: \"The wind clawed at her coat.\"", "why": "Makes a clear point, names the technique and quotes briefly. This is the starting point, not the whole answer."}, {"do": "The verb \"clawed\" suggests sharp claws, so the wind seems like an aggressive animal.", "why": "Zooms in on one word and explains what it suggests. This is the analysis the marks reward."}, {"do": "This makes the reader feel she is in danger, creating tension.", "why": "Links the word to the effect on the reader, which is what the question asks for."}, {"do": "Similarly, \"swallowing her footsteps\" suggests the dark devours sound, making the lane threatening and isolating.", "why": "Adds a second, different quotation and explains its effect, which widens the range of the answer."}], "answer": "The writer presents the wind as hostile through personification: \"The wind clawed at her coat.\" The verb \"clawed\" suggests sharp claws, so the wind seems like an aggressive animal. This makes the reader feel she is in danger, creating tension. Similarly, \"swallowing her footsteps\" suggests the dark devours sound, making the lane threatening and isolating."}, "selfx": {"q": "In the second step, the answer focuses on the single word \"clawed\". Why does it do this?", "o": ["Only verbs can be analysed, so the other words in the quotation must be ignored", "One word always earns more marks than a longer quotation, whatever is said about it", "That word carries the meaning, so exploring what it suggests shows close analysis", "One word speaks for itself, so no explanation of its effect is needed"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Other word types can be analysed too. Verbs are often a good choice, but this is not a rule that bars the rest.", "Wrong. Marks come from the quality of the explanation, not from the length of the quotation. A short quote with no analysis earns little.", "Right. 'Clawed' links to animals and pain, so exploring it shows what the writer's choice suggests. That detail is what earns the marks.", "Wrong. No word explains itself. You must always say what it suggests and how it affects the reader."]}, "faded": {"task": "Read: \"Rain hammered the roof. Inside, the kitchen was a cage of silence.\" How does the writer use language to create atmosphere? Choose the best next sentence.", "given": ["The writer creates a violent, uneasy atmosphere. The storm is shown through the verb in \"Rain hammered the roof.\""], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn the most marks?", "o": ["'Hammered' is a verb, a doing word, so it shows what the rain does to the roof.", "'Hammered' suggests heavy, repeated blows, so the rain seems forceful and relentless.", "The writer says the rain hammered the roof to describe the weather in a lot of detail.", "'Hammered' is onomatopoeia, which makes the writing more interesting for the reader."], "a": 1, "f": ["This only gives the word class. It does not say what the word suggests, so there is no analysis.", "Right. It zooms in on 'hammered' and explains what it suggests (heavy, repeated blows). The effect on the reader can follow.", "This repeats the quotation in other words. It says nothing about what the word suggests, so it is description, not analysis.", "This names a technique, then gives a vague effect ('more interesting'). It does not explain what these particular words suggest."]}, "trap": "Naming a technique, such as personification, and stopping. Always add the specific words and what they suggest. Test yourself: have you written 'this suggests…' or 'this makes the reader…'?", "recap": "Quote a few words, zoom in on one, then explain what it suggests and how it affects the reader."}
{"s": "english_lang", "t": "el-p2-q5a", "kind": "writing", "why": "Question 5 is worth 40 marks, half of Paper 2, and asks you to argue a view for a stated audience in a form such as a letter, article or speech.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Fix audience, purpose, form", "b": "Before you write, decide three things. The **audience** is who reads it. The **purpose** is usually to argue or persuade. The **form** is a letter, article or speech. Together they set your tone. A headteacher needs formal Standard English. A speech to students can be warmer and more direct."}, {"h": "State and develop points", "b": "Say your **viewpoint** clearly in the opening. Then give each point its own paragraph. Do not stop at the opinion. **Develop** it with a reason, an example or a result. \"Homework is too long\" is only a claim. Add what it causes, and the point starts to persuade."}, {"h": "Answer the other side", "b": "A **counter-argument** is a view that opposes yours. Raise it fairly: \"Some may argue... but...\". Then **answer** it with a reason. This shows you have thought about both sides, and it makes your own case stronger. One short paragraph is enough."}, {"h": "Techniques and accuracy", "b": "Use techniques on purpose, not in every sentence: **direct address**, a rhetorical question, a list of three, a short fact or statistic. End with a strong closing line. Marks also go on **technical accuracy**: spelling, punctuation and varied sentences."}], "example": {"task": "Write a letter to your headteacher, Mr Hughes, arguing that the school library should stay open until 5 pm. A model answer is built below in four parts.", "steps": [{"do": "Dear Mr Hughes, I am writing to urge you to keep the library open until 5 pm.", "why": "Formal greeting suits the audience. It states the purpose and the writer's view straight away."}, {"do": "Many of us share noisy homes, so quiet study is hard. A calm library lets us revise properly.", "why": "The point is developed: a reason (noisy homes) and a result (better revision), not just an opinion."}, {"do": "Some may argue it costs too much. But staff are already on site, so the cost is small.", "why": "It raises the opposing view fairly, then answers it with a reason. This shows balance and strengthens the case."}, {"do": "Surely better exam results are worth it?", "why": "A short rhetorical question links back to the revision point and ends firmly on the writer's view."}], "answer": "Dear Mr Hughes, I am writing to urge you to keep the library open until 5 pm. Many of us share noisy homes, so quiet study is hard. A calm library lets us revise properly. Some may argue it costs too much. But staff are already on site, so the cost is small. Surely better exam results are worth it?"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 add \"But staff are already on site\" after the opposing view?", "o": ["It repeats the opening request in different words.", "It answers the opposing view with a reason, so the case looks stronger.", "It switches the audience from the headteacher to the staff.", "It agrees the library should close, to seem fair."], "a": 1, "f": ["The sentence gives a new reason (staff are already there). It does not repeat the request from the opening.", "Right. Raising a counter-argument is not enough. You must answer it with a reason, which makes your own view more convincing.", "The audience is still Mr Hughes. The staff are mentioned as evidence, not addressed.", "The writer still argues for the library staying open. Being fair means admitting the other view exists, not giving in to it."]}, "faded": {"task": "Write a speech for Year 10 arguing that students should volunteer in their community.", "given": ["Opening: \"Good morning, everyone. Today I want to persuade you that volunteering is worth your time.\"", "Point 1: \"It builds skills. Organising a charity event teaches teamwork that no lesson can match.\""], "q": "Which sentence should come next to raise and answer a counter-argument?", "o": ["Volunteering is also good fun, and it makes you feel happy and proud.", "Some people say they are too busy, but honestly they are just being lazy.", "Of course, nobody has time for volunteering, so I understand if you disagree.", "Some may argue that we are too busy. But one hour a week is easy to fit in."], "a": 3, "f": ["This is another opinion in support. It does not raise the opposing view or answer it.", "It raises the other side but only insults it. There is no reason, and the tone could put off the audience.", "It raises the other side but gives in to it. The writer must answer the objection, not agree with it.", "Right. It states the opposing view fairly with \"Some may argue\", then answers it with a reason: one hour a week is small."]}, "trap": "Writing a list of opinions (\"It's unfair. It's boring.\") without reasons. Fix: after each point, add \"because\" and a result or example. Match your tone to the reader: formal for a headteacher, no slang.", "recap": "Fix audience, purpose and form first, then state your view, develop every point, and answer a counter-argument in a tone that suits the reader."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-nv-cc", "kind": "writing", "why": "Paper 1 Section B tests Dickens's ideas and methods, and a tight paragraph built on a short quotation is the core of every answer.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Start with a clear point", "b": "Begin with a **point** that answers the question about Dickens's **ideas**. Say what he presents and what it shows about society. Do not retell the plot. 'Dickens presents Scrooge as…' beats 'Then the ghost takes Scrooge to…'."}, {"h": "Quote, method, effect", "b": "Use a short **quotation**, only a few words. Name the **method**, such as an imperative, a simile, personification or a symbol. Then explain the **effect** on the reader. All three together make analysis."}, {"h": "Link to context and purpose", "b": "Add **context** only when it explains the idea. The novella came out in 1843. Many poor children had little food or schooling, and the 1834 Poor Law pushed the poor towards workhouses. End on Dickens's **purpose**: what he wants readers to think or do."}], "example": {"task": "Explore how Dickens presents poverty in A Christmas Carol. Write one analytical paragraph about the novella as a whole.", "steps": [{"do": "Dickens presents poverty as a danger the rich can still prevent.", "why": "A clear point answers the question directly and sets the idea you will prove."}, {"do": "The Ghost's imperative, 'Beware them both', about Ignorance and Want, two personified children, makes the warning urgent and speaks to readers as well as Scrooge.", "why": "A short quotation is evidence. Naming the method and its effect shows how Dickens's language works."}, {"do": "Many poor Victorian children lacked food and schooling, so the warning felt real.", "why": "Context earns marks when it explains why the idea mattered to Dickens's readers."}, {"do": "Scrooge's final generosity shows the rich can act, as Dickens urges.", "why": "Linking to the whole novella and Dickens's purpose shows you understand the book as a whole."}], "answer": "Dickens presents poverty as a danger the rich can still prevent. The Ghost's imperative, 'Beware them both', about Ignorance and Want, two personified children, makes the warning urgent and speaks to readers as well as Scrooge. Many poor Victorian children lacked food and schooling, so the warning felt real. Scrooge's final generosity shows the rich can act, as Dickens urges."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model paragraph name the 'imperative' when it quotes the Ghost?", "o": ["The mark scheme demands one technical term in every sentence", "It shows how Dickens's language creates an effect on readers", "It makes the quotation look longer and more impressive", "It proves the quotation has been copied out accurately"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. No such rule exists. A term earns marks only when you use it to explain an effect.", "Right. Naming the method and saying what it does shows you are analysing Dickens's choices, not just quoting.", "Wrong. Length earns nothing. The quotation is kept short so that your comment on it can carry the marks.", "Wrong. Accuracy matters, but naming a method has nothing to do with checking a quotation."], "x": null}, "faded": {"task": "Explore how Dickens presents Scrooge at the start of the novella. Write one analytical paragraph.", "given": ["Dickens presents Scrooge as cold and isolated by choice.", "He is described as 'solitary as an oyster'."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to earn the most marks?", "o": ["Dickens uses a simile here, which is a good technique to use.", "Scrooge is very unkind and the reader dislikes him a lot.", "The simile suggests he shuts himself away, like a closed shell, by choice.", "Later, the Ghost of Christmas Past shows him sitting alone at school."], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. It names the method but gives no effect. Say what the simile suggests about Scrooge.", "Wrong. This is vague. It names no method and does not link to the quotation.", "Right. It names the simile, explains the closed-shell image and links it to Scrooge choosing isolation.", "Wrong. This retells the plot and leaves the quotation. Analyse the evidence you have just given."]}, "trap": "Retelling the plot instead of analysing. After every quotation, name Dickens's method and explain its effect on the reader. If a sentence only says what happens, rewrite it to say what Dickens shows.", "recap": "Make a point about Dickens's ideas, support it with a short quotation, analyse the method and effect, then link to context and purpose."}
{"s": "english_lit", "t": "lit-sh-mac", "kind": "writing", "why": "Section A of Paper 1 is a Macbeth question worth 34 of the 64 marks, and it asks you to write about an extract and the whole play.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What earns marks", "b": "Examiners reward three things. First, a clear **personal response** to the question, backed by quotations. Second, analysis of Shakespeare's **methods**: language, structure and form. Third, **context**, used only where it explains an idea. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are marked too."}, {"h": "Build each paragraph", "b": "Start with a **point** that answers the question. Add a short **quotation**. Then pick out a key word or method and explain its **effect** on the audience. Quotations of a few words work best, because you can analyse every word."}, {"h": "Use the whole play", "b": "The question covers an extract and the play as a whole. Begin with the extract, then link to a moment earlier or later. A **contrast** or a **change** between two moments shows that you understand the **structure** of the play."}, {"h": "Weave in context", "b": "Context is a short link, not a separate paragraph. Many in **Jacobean** England, including **King James I**, believed a king was chosen by God. So killing one was a terrible sin. Use this to explain how an audience would react."}], "example": {"task": "Starting with Act 5 Scene 1, where she sleepwalks, explore how Shakespeare presents Lady Macbeth's guilt. Write about this scene and the play as a whole.", "steps": [{"do": "Shakespeare presents Lady Macbeth's guilt as a punishment she cannot escape, as her early confidence collapses into torment.", "why": "A clear argument answers the question straight away."}, {"do": "In Act 2 she dismisses guilt: \"A little water clears us of this deed.\" The short, simple words suggest she believes murder can be washed away easily.", "why": "A short quotation from earlier in the play, with the effect of the language explained."}, {"do": "By Act 5 she sleepwalks, rubbing her hands and crying \"Out, damned spot!\" The imperative \"Out\" shows her trying to command away a stain that is only imagined, so the torment is in her mind.", "why": "The contrast with Act 2 shows change. A method is named and its effect explained."}, {"do": "A Jacobean audience, who saw killing a king as a sin against God, would see her breakdown as deserved punishment, not just sadness.", "why": "Context explains the audience's reaction and is tied to the argument."}], "answer": "Shakespeare shows that Lady Macbeth's belief that guilt is easily washed away is false. The \"little water\" of Act 2 becomes the stain she can never remove in Act 5."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer quote from Act 2 before the quote from Act 5?", "o": ["Quotations must always appear in the order they are printed in the text.", "It lets the answer show how her attitude changes across the play.", "Quotations from early in the play earn more marks than later ones.", "It makes the answer longer, and longer answers always score higher."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Order is not a rule. Here the order works because it traces a change, and you could use a different order if your argument needed it.", "Right. The Act 2 confidence sets up a contrast with the Act 5 breakdown. That shows development across the whole play, which the question asks for.", "Wrong. The mark scheme does not reward quotations by position in the play. It rewards how well they support and develop your argument.", "Wrong. Length alone earns nothing. Marks go for the quality of ideas and analysis, not the number of words."]}, "faded": {"task": "Starting with Act 3 Scene 1, explore how Shakespeare presents Macbeth's fear of Banquo. Write about this scene and the play as a whole.", "given": ["Opening: Shakespeare presents Banquo as a threat that stops Macbeth enjoying being king.", "Point: In Act 3 Scene 1, Macbeth is king but feels unsafe because of Banquo."], "q": "Which sentence should come next?", "o": ["Next, Macbeth hires murderers to kill Banquo, but Fleance escapes.", "Shakespeare uses powerful language here, which makes the audience feel scared.", "He says \"To be thus is nothing\", showing his crown feels worthless without safety.", "He says \"To be thus is nothing\", which is a good quote about Banquo and fear."], "a": 2, "f": ["This retells the plot. It moves on without a quotation or any analysis of Shakespeare's methods, so it earns little.", "This is vague. It names no technique, gives no quotation and does not say which words create the effect, so there is nothing to analyse.", "Right. It adds a short quotation and explains it. The word \"nothing\" shows that being king feels worthless while Banquo makes Macbeth feel unsafe.", "This has a quotation but no analysis. Calling it \"good\" explains nothing. You need to say what the words show and how they do it."]}, "trap": "Retelling what happens (\"then she sleepwalks\") instead of analysing. Marks go for how Shakespeare presents ideas. After every quotation, pick out a key word or method and explain its effect on the audience.", "recap": "Make a clear point, support it with a short quotation, analyse a key word or method, and link to another moment or to context."}
{"s": "history", "t": "el-1", "kind": "idea", "why": "OCR questions ask how far Elizabeth controlled her government and country, so you need specific evidence of her power and its limits.", "hook": {"q": "Elizabeth had no police force and only a tiny permanent army. How was she most likely to get her orders carried out in the counties?", "o": ["By sending royal officials from London to run every county", "By relying on local landowners to act for her, without pay", "By letting each county choose its own ruler", "By asking Parliament to run local affairs for her"], "a": 1, "f": ["She did not have enough paid officials or money for this. Most local work was done by people who already lived in the counties.", "Right. The crown depended on local gentry to enforce her laws and keep order. This is the idea behind Justices of the Peace.", "Counties did not choose their own rulers. Authority came from the queen, and local leaders acted in her name.", "Parliament met rarely and was not a body that ran local affairs. Local government was done by gentry on her behalf."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Court and Privy Council", "b": "The **court** was the queen's household and the centre of power. Nobles competed there for **patronage**: offices, land and monopolies. The **Privy Council** was a small group of advisers she chose, such as William Cecil. It met often and carried out government business. Elizabeth made the final decisions."}, {"h": "Parliament and Puritans", "b": "Only Elizabeth could call or dismiss **Parliament**, and she could veto its laws. It met rarely, mainly to vote taxes. **Puritan** MPs pressed for further religious reform. She refused to let MPs debate religion or the succession, and jailed critics such as Peter Wentworth in 1593. She made concessions over monopolies in 1601."}, {"h": "Local rule and propaganda", "b": "She had no police force. **Justices of the Peace** (JPs), unpaid local gentry, enforced laws in the counties. **Propaganda** built loyalty. Portraits showed her as a strong Virgin Queen, and summer progresses let ordinary people see her. Her hosts paid much of the cost."}, {"h": "Essex's rebellion, 1601", "b": "The Earl of **Essex** lost favour after failing in Ireland, and his monopoly on sweet wines was not renewed. He blamed Robert **Cecil** and rose in London on 8 February 1601. Almost nobody joined him. He was executed on 25 February. It showed court rivalry was dangerous, but she still held control."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why Elizabeth was able to keep control of England in the 1590s.", "steps": [{"do": "Elizabeth chose her own Privy Council, including the Cecils, and rewarded loyal nobles with offices, land and monopolies. The Council advised her, but she made the final decisions.", "why": "Shows power at the centre came from her, and that favour kept nobles loyal."}, {"do": "Parliament could not seriously challenge her. Only she could call or dismiss it, and she could veto its laws. She jailed critics such as Peter Wentworth in 1593.", "why": "Shows how she contained challenges from MPs, a second source of her control."}, {"do": "In the counties, she relied on JPs. These unpaid gentry enforced her laws, because she had no police force. They wanted to keep her favour.", "why": "Explains how her orders reached the whole country cheaply."}, {"do": "Propaganda gave her support. Portraits showed a strong Virgin Queen, and progresses let people see her. Loyal subjects were less likely to rebel, and Essex found almost no support in 1601.", "why": "Links loyalty to a real event as evidence."}], "answer": "Elizabeth kept control because she held the key powers. She chose her councillors and gave out patronage. She decided when Parliament met. Gentry as JPs enforced her rule locally, and propaganda built loyalty, so Essex's rebellion in 1601 failed."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention that only she could call or dismiss Parliament and could veto laws?", "o": ["It shows Parliament had no part in government at all", "It shows Parliament met every year to check her power", "It shows she ruled mainly through laws made by the Commons", "It shows Parliament depended on her and could not overrule her"], "a": 3, "f": ["Too strong. Parliament did vote taxes and pass laws. The point is that it worked under her control, not that it did nothing.", "Parliament met rarely, and only when she chose to call it. It was not a regular check on her.", "Wrong emphasis. She governed through her Council and royal power. Parliament met rarely, and she could veto its laws.", "Right. Her control of when Parliament met, and of which laws passed, shows it could not overrule her. This supports the point about her power."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how Elizabeth's government kept control of the counties in the 1590s.", "given": ["Point 1: Elizabeth had no police force or large standing army, so she relied on local gentry to carry out her rule."], "q": "Which is the best next point?", "o": ["Point 2: Parliament chose the JPs and ran each county for her.", "Point 2: JPs were elected by local people and paid wages by the crown.", "Point 2: JPs came from the local gentry, were unpaid and enforced her laws in their county.", "Point 2: The Council sent soldiers from London to garrison every county town."], "a": 2, "f": ["This is the trap. Parliament did not choose JPs or run the counties. The crown appointed them, and the Privy Council supervised them.", "JPs were appointed by the crown, not elected. They were not paid wages. They served for the status and to keep the queen's favour.", "Right. This follows from Point 1 and names who did the work. JPs tried minor crimes and enforced laws, and they served without pay.", "She had no large paid army to station in towns. The point is that the government relied on local gentry rather than on soldiers."]}, "trap": "Saying Parliament chose the Privy Council or ran the country. Elizabeth chose her councillors, decided when Parliament met and made the final decisions. Avoid this by naming who held each power.", "recap": "Elizabeth held final power: she chose her Council, controlled Parliament, and relied on nobles and gentry to carry out her rule locally."}
{"s": "history", "t": "ph-1", "kind": "writing", "why": "The People's Health paper often asks you to explain why people acted as they did, which needs their beliefs plus specific evidence.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Living conditions", "b": "Medieval towns were **crowded**, with narrow streets. People dumped rubbish, human waste and animal guts in the streets and rivers. Rivers such as the Thames and Fleet became **polluted**. Nobody knew about **germs**, so few saw the danger."}, {"h": "Beliefs about causes", "b": "People explained disease in three main ways. **Miasma**: bad air from rotting waste. The **four humours**: an imbalance of body fluids. **God's punishment**: sin made God angry. Each belief led to different actions, so reactions to the Black Death varied."}, {"h": "Public health efforts", "b": "Towns ordered streets cleaned, built **conduits** to bring in spring water, and in 1388 Parliament banned dumping waste in ditches and rivers. These laws were often poorly enforced. **Monasteries** often did better, with piped water and latrines built over streams."}, {"h": "Building an explanation", "b": "Each paragraph needs a **point** that answers the 'why'. Back it with **evidence**: a named belief, group or action. Finish with a **link** back to the question. Name the belief, then show the action it led to."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why people reacted to the Black Death of 1348–49 in so many different ways.", "steps": [{"do": "People reacted in different ways because nobody knew the real cause of the plague, so they blamed different things.", "why": "It answers 'why' straight away and gives your whole answer a line of argument."}, {"do": "Many believed God sent the plague to punish sin. So flagellants whipped themselves in public to win God's forgiveness, and others prayed.", "why": "A named group and belief explain the action. 'To win God's forgiveness' shows it was not a cure."}, {"do": "Others believed in miasma, bad air from rotting waste. So towns ordered streets cleaned and people carried sweet-smelling herbs to cover bad smells.", "why": "It names the belief, then shows the actions that followed from it."}, {"do": "Doctors followed the four humours, so they used bloodletting to remove extra blood and restore balance.", "why": "A third belief gives a third kind of reaction, so the answer covers a range."}, {"do": "All of these made sense at the time. Fleas carried by rats spread the plague and nobody knew it, so different beliefs led to different actions.", "why": "The link ties the evidence back to the question and gives the underlying reason."}], "answer": "A full answer gives an overall reason, then three beliefs (God's punishment, miasma, humours), each with the action it caused, and ends by linking to ignorance of the true cause."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 say flagellants whipped themselves 'to win God's forgiveness'?", "o": ["Whipping was a medical treatment meant to drain the plague from the body", "Flagellants were doctors who whipped patients to remove bad air", "It shows their action came from the belief that plague was God's punishment", "Whipping was ordered by town councils as a way to clean the streets"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Whipping was not a medical treatment. That mixes flagellants up with bloodletting and the humours.", "Wrong. Flagellants whipped themselves, not patients, and they were not doctors. Bad air belongs to miasma.", "Right. The purpose, forgiveness, links the action to the belief in God's anger. That explains the reaction and earns the mark.", "Wrong. Flagellants acted on their own for religious reasons. Street cleaning was a separate response based on miasma."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why monasteries were often cleaner than medieval towns.", "given": ["Point: Monasteries had the money and organisation to plan their water and waste."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to give specific evidence for this point?", "o": ["Monks cleaned because they knew germs caused disease, so they boiled all their water.", "Many had piped fresh water and latrines built over a stream that carried waste away.", "Monasteries were very old, and many monks lived there for their whole lives.", "Monks never caught the plague because God protected them for living holy lives."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Germs were not understood until much later. Monks cleaned for other reasons, such as order and comfort.", "Right. It gives specific evidence of water and waste systems, which supports the point and explains the cleaner conditions.", "Wrong. This does not explain cleanliness. It is detail with no link to the question.", "Wrong. This is inaccurate, since monks died of plague too. It also drifts from cleanliness to belief."]}, "trap": "Writing that flagellants tried to cure the sick, or that medieval people did nothing about health. Flagellants did penance for sin. Towns and monasteries did act, but without knowing the real causes.", "recap": "Explain reactions by naming the belief, such as miasma, humours or God's punishment, then the action it caused, because nobody knew about fleas or germs."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "A4", "kind": "method", "why": "Expanding and factorising appear on all three papers and are needed for solving quadratics, and surd simplifying appears in non-calculator questions.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Expanding two brackets", "b": "Multiply **every term** in the first bracket by every term in the second. $(x+a)(x+b)$ gives four terms. Then collect the **like terms**, usually the two $x$ terms in the middle. Take care with signs: $3\\times(-5)=-15$."}, {"h": "Factorising $x^2+bx+c$", "b": "Find two numbers that **multiply** to give $c$ and **add** to give $b$. Call them $p$ and $q$. Then $x^2+bx+c=(x+p)(x+q)$. List the factor pairs of $c$ and test each pair's sum."}, {"h": "Which signs to use", "b": "If $c$ is **positive**, both numbers have the same sign as $b$. If $c$ is **negative**, one number is positive and one is negative. The sum is then the difference of the two, and the larger number takes the sign of $b$."}, {"h": "Surds and checking", "b": "For **surds**, $\\sqrt{a}\\times\\sqrt{a}=a$, and you simplify by taking out a **square number**: $\\sqrt{12}=\\sqrt{4}\\times\\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}$. Always check a factorisation by expanding it. You should get the original expression back."}], "example": {"task": "Factorise $x^2+2x-24$.", "steps": [{"do": "Look at $c=-24$. It is negative, so the two numbers have opposite signs.", "why": "A negative product needs one positive and one negative number."}, {"do": "List the factor pairs of 24: $1\\times24$, $2\\times12$, $3\\times8$, $4\\times6$.", "why": "The two numbers must multiply to 24 in size, so they come from this list."}, {"do": "The numbers have opposite signs, so the sum is their difference. Look for a difference of 2. That is 4 and 6.", "why": "We need them to add to $b=+2$. Only the pair 4 and 6 differs by 2."}, {"do": "The larger number, 6, takes the sign of $b$, which is $+$. So the numbers are $+6$ and $-4$.", "why": "Check: $6\\times(-4)=-24$ and $6+(-4)=2$. Both conditions hold."}, {"do": "Write the brackets: $(x+6)(x-4)$.", "why": "The numbers $p=6$ and $q=-4$ go straight into $(x+p)(x+q)$."}, {"do": "Check by expanding: $x^2-4x+6x-24=x^2+2x-24$.", "why": "Getting the original expression back shows the factorisation is correct."}], "answer": "$x^2+2x-24=(x+6)(x-4)$"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is 6 made positive and 4 made negative in the worked example?", "o": ["The bigger number always goes in the first bracket, so it must be positive", "The numbers must add to $+2$, so the larger one has to be positive", "The product is $-24$, so both numbers must be negative", "The factor 6 is even, so it takes the plus sign"], "a": 1, "f": ["Order of brackets does not decide signs. The sum of $+2$ does, so the larger number must be positive.", "Right. A sum of $+2$ means the larger number wins the sign. So $+6$ and $-4$ add to $+2$.", "Two negatives would multiply to a positive, not $-24$. One number must be positive and one negative.", "Being even has no effect on a sign. The sign comes from the product and the sum."]}, "faded": {"task": "Factorise $x^2-2x-15$.", "given": ["$c=-15$ is negative, so the numbers have opposite signs.", "Factor pairs of 15: $1\\times15$ and $3\\times5$. The pair with difference 2 is 3 and 5."], "q": "Which numbers should go in the brackets next?", "o": ["$-3$ and $+5$", "$+3$ and $+5$", "$+3$ and $-5$", "$-3$ and $-5$"], "a": 2, "f": ["These multiply to $-15$ but add to $+2$. We need $b=-2$, so the larger number, 5, must be negative.", "These multiply to $+15$, not $-15$. Opposite signs are needed because $c$ is negative.", "Right. $3\\times(-5)=-15$ and $3+(-5)=-2$. So $x^2-2x-15=(x+3)(x-5)$.", "These multiply to $+15$ and add to $-8$. Two negatives cannot give a product of $-15$."]}, "trap": "Picking numbers with the right product but the wrong sum, such as $+5$ instead of $-5$. Test both the product and the sum, then expand your brackets to check.", "recap": "To factorise $x^2+bx+c$, find two numbers that multiply to $c$ and add to $b$, then check by expanding."}
{"s": "maths", "t": "R9", "kind": "method", "why": "Percentage change questions can appear on any of your three Edexcel papers, and reverse percentages are a common place to lose marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Percentage change", "b": "To find a **percentage change**, work out the change first. Then divide by the **original** value and multiply by 100.\n\n$\\text{percentage change}=\\dfrac{\\text{change}}{\\text{original}}\\times100$\n\nThe original is the value before the change. A rise from £50 to £62 is £12, so $12\\div50\\times100=24\\%$."}, {"h": "Multipliers and over 100%", "b": "A **multiplier** does a whole percentage change in one step.\n\nIncrease by 15%: $100\\%+15\\%=115\\%$, so the multiplier is $1.15$.\nDecrease by 15%: $100\\%-15\\%=85\\%$, so the multiplier is $0.85$.\n\nA **percentage over 100%** just means bigger than the original. 130% of a number is $1.3$ times it."}, {"h": "Finding the original value", "b": "New value $=$ original $\\times$ multiplier. To go back, **divide by the multiplier**.\n\nDo not find a percentage of the new value and add or subtract it. The percentage was taken of the **original**, which you do not know yet."}, {"h": "Simple interest", "b": "**Simple interest** is the same amount every year. It is worked out from the **original sum** only.\n\nInterest per year $=$ original sum $\\times$ rate $\\div100$. Multiply by the number of years.\n\nExample: £400 at 3% for 5 years. $400\\times0.03=12$, so £12 a year. The interest is $12\\times5=60$, so £60."}], "example": {"task": "In a sale, all bikes are reduced by 35%. One bike has a sale price of £260. Work out the original price of the bike.", "steps": [{"do": "The original price is 100%. After a 35% reduction, the sale price is $100\\%-35\\%=65\\%$ of the original.", "why": "This tells you what percentage of the original the known £260 is."}, {"do": "Write 65% as the multiplier $0.65$. So original $\\times\\,0.65=260$.", "why": "A multiplier turns the original price into the sale price."}, {"do": "Divide by the multiplier: original $=260\\div0.65$.", "why": "Dividing undoes multiplying by 0.65."}, {"do": "Calculate: $260\\div0.65=26000\\div65=400$.", "why": "Multiplying both numbers by 100 removes the decimal and makes it easier without a calculator."}, {"do": "Check: 35% of 400 is 140, and $400-140=260$.", "why": "Going forward from the original should give the sale price you were told."}], "answer": "The original price was £400."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 3 divide £260 by 0.65 instead of taking 35% off £260?", "o": ["Dividing by a multiplier is always the way to answer any percentage question", "The 35% should be taken off £260 first, and then 0.65 is used to finish", "£260 is 65% of the original, so dividing by 0.65 undoes the reduction", "Dividing by 0.65 is the same as adding 35% of £260 to £260"], "a": 2, "f": ["Not always. You multiply by the multiplier to find a new value from a known original. You divide only when the new value is known and the original is not.", "There is no first step of taking 35% off £260. The 35% belongs to the unknown original, so the multiplier does the whole job alone.", "Right. The sale price is original × 0.65. Dividing the sale price by 0.65 reverses this and gives the original.", "This is a common slip. Adding 35% of £260 gives £351, not £400. A percentage of the new value is not the same as the same percentage of the original."]}, "faded": {"task": "A monthly phone plan rises by 12% to £56. Work out the price before the rise.", "given": ["An increase of 12% means the new price is 100% + 12% = 112% of the original.", "The multiplier is 1.12, so original × 1.12 = 56."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Work out $56\\times1.12$", "Work out $56\\div1.12$", "Work out 12% of 56 and subtract it", "Work out $56\\div0.12$"], "a": 1, "f": ["This multiplies the new price by 1.12, which would add 12% again. You need to undo the increase, so you divide.", "Correct. Dividing by the multiplier undoes the increase: $56\\div1.12=50$. The original price was £50.", "This takes 12% of the new price. The 12% was of the original, so this gives £49.28, which is wrong.", "0.12 is only the 12% on its own. The multiplier for a 12% increase is 1.12, because the original 100% is included."]}, "trap": "Taking the percentage off the new value, for example finding 12% of £56 and subtracting it. The percentage was of the original. Write the multiplier, then divide the new value by it.", "recap": "To find the original value, divide the new value by the multiplier. Never take a percentage of the new value."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.1.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Energy questions appear on Paper 1, and kinetic energy and power are often combined in one multi-mark calculation.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Energy stores", "b": "Energy is held in **stores**. The stores are kinetic, gravitational potential, elastic potential, thermal, chemical, nuclear, magnetic and electrostatic. A moving object has energy in its **kinetic** store. A raised object has energy in its **gravitational potential** store. A hot object has energy in its thermal store."}, {"h": "Energy transfers", "b": "Energy moves between stores by heating, by waves, by an electric current, or by a force doing **work**. In a closed system the total energy is **conserved**. It is never created or destroyed. Energy not usefully transferred mostly ends up in the thermal store of the surroundings. This is **wasted** energy."}, {"h": "Two store equations", "b": "**Kinetic energy**: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$. **Gravitational potential energy**: $E_p = mgh$. Use mass in kg, speed in m/s and height in m. Here g = 9.8 N/kg. Energy is in **joules** (J). Check every unit before you substitute."}, {"h": "Power", "b": "**Power** is how fast energy is transferred: $P = \\frac{E}{t}$. Use E in joules and t in **seconds**. The unit is the **watt** (W). 1 W means 1 joule transferred every second. A 60 W bulb transfers 60 J each second."}], "example": {"task": "A sprinter of mass 60 kg accelerates from rest to 8.0 m/s in 4.0 s. Assume all the energy transferred goes to her kinetic store. Calculate the power of the transfer.", "steps": [{"do": "List what you know: m = 60 kg, v = 8.0 m/s, t = 4.0 s. The store gaining energy is kinetic.", "why": "This shows which equation to use. The units are already kg, m/s and s."}, {"do": "Write the equation: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$.", "why": "Kinetic energy depends on mass and speed."}, {"do": "Square the speed first: $8.0^2 = 64$.", "why": "Only v is squared, so deal with it before anything else."}, {"do": "Multiply by the mass, then halve: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 60 \\times 64 = 1920$ J.", "why": "She starts at rest, so all of this is the energy transferred."}, {"do": "Write $P = \\frac{E}{t}$ and substitute: $P = \\frac{1920}{4.0}$.", "why": "Power is energy transferred per second. The time is already in seconds."}, {"do": "Calculate: $P = 480$ W.", "why": "Joules per second are watts, so 480 W means 480 J each second."}], "answer": "Energy transferred = 1920 J. Power = 480 W."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer square 8.0 before multiplying by 60?", "o": ["Because the whole of ½ × 60 × 8.0 must be squared at the end", "Because squaring turns metres per second into joules", "Because in the equation only the speed is squared, not the ½ or the mass", "Because the power equation also needs the speed squared"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Squaring the whole product gives (240)² = 57 600, which is far too big. The square belongs to v only.", "Wrong. Squaring does not change units. The joules come from kg × (m/s)², all together.", "Right. In ½mv², the power 2 applies only to v. Square v first, then multiply by m and halve.", "Wrong. P = E/t has no speed in it. The square belongs to the kinetic energy equation."]}, "faded": {"task": "A 4.0 kg trolley is pushed from rest to 3.0 m/s in 2.5 s. All the energy goes to its kinetic store. Calculate the power.", "given": ["Kinetic energy: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$", "Substitute: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4.0 \\times 3.0^2$"], "q": "What is the correct next step?", "o": ["$E_k = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4.0 \\times 6.0 = 12$ J", "$3.0^2 = 9.0$, so $E_k = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4.0 \\times 9.0 = 18$ J", "$E_k = (\\frac{1}{2} \\times 4.0 \\times 3.0)^2 = 36$ J", "$E_k = 4.0 \\times 9.0 = 36$ J"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. This doubles the speed (3.0 × 2) instead of squaring it. Squaring means 3.0 × 3.0 = 9.0.", "Right. Square the speed first, then multiply by the mass and halve. Power is then 18 ÷ 2.5 = 7.2 W.", "Wrong. This squares after multiplying by ½ and m. Only the speed should be squared.", "Wrong. Squaring is correct here, but the ½ has been left out. Kinetic energy needs the half."]}, "trap": "Squaring the wrong thing, or not at all. Write the square of v as its own line first. Also keep time in seconds: convert minutes before using P = E/t.", "recap": "Square v, multiply by m, halve to get kinetic energy in joules; divide by the time in seconds to get power in watts."}
{"s": "physics", "t": "4.2.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Calculations with $V = IR$ come up in Paper 1, often for 2 or 3 marks, and you also need to know how resistors, lamps and diodes behave.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Setting up the circuit", "b": "Connect the **ammeter** in **series** with the component, so the same current flows through it. Connect the **voltmeter** in parallel, directly across the component only. Read both meters, then use them to find the resistance."}, {"h": "Using V = IR", "b": "The equation is $V = IR$, so $R = \\frac{V}{I}$ and $I = \\frac{V}{R}$. Use **volts** (V), amps (A) and **ohms** (Ω). Convert **milliamps** to amps by dividing by 1000. For resistance, V always goes on top."}, {"h": "Resistor, lamp and diode", "b": "A fixed **resistor** at constant temperature gives a straight-line I–V graph through the origin. Its resistance stays the same. In a **filament lamp** the wire heats up as current rises, so resistance increases and the graph curves. A **diode** lets current flow in one direction only. Its resistance is very high the other way."}], "example": {"task": "A student sets up a circuit to test a fixed resistor. The voltmeter reads 6.0 V and the ammeter reads 25 mA. Calculate the resistance of the resistor.", "steps": [{"do": "Write down what you know: V = 6.0 V across the resistor, I = 25 mA through it.", "why": "The voltmeter gives the potential difference. The ammeter gives the current."}, {"do": "Convert the current to amps: 25 mA = 25 ÷ 1000 = 0.025 A.", "why": "The equation needs amps to give an answer in ohms."}, {"do": "Write the equation: $R = \\frac{V}{I}$.", "why": "Resistance is wanted, so V goes on top and I underneath."}, {"do": "Substitute and calculate: $R = \\frac{6.0}{0.025} = 240$.", "why": "Both values are now in the correct units."}, {"do": "State the unit: R = 240 Ω.", "why": "Resistance is measured in ohms. A missing unit can lose a mark."}], "answer": "R = 240 Ω"}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does step 2 change 25 mA into 0.025 A?", "o": ["The voltmeter reading is in volts, so the current must be changed to volts too.", "The equation R = V ÷ I only gives ohms when the current is in amps.", "An ammeter can only ever show amps, so milliamps must be converted.", "Dividing by 1000 makes the answer smaller, which is always needed."], "a": 1, "f": ["Volts and amps are different quantities. A current is never converted into volts. The point is to use the unit the equation is built on.", "Right. V in volts and I in amps give R in ohms. Using 25 instead of 0.025 would give an answer 1000 times too small.", "Ammeters can show milliamps. The conversion is needed because the equation is defined with current in amps, not because of the meter.", "The conversion is not about the size of the answer. Skipping it gives a wrong answer, and it would not always make the answer smaller."]}, "faded": {"task": "A 470 Ω resistor has a current of 12 mA through it. Calculate the potential difference across it.", "given": ["Current: I = 12 mA = 0.012 A (divide by 1000)", "Equation: V = IR (V is wanted, so multiply I by R)"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["V = 470 ÷ 0.012 = about 39 000 V", "V = 12 × 470 = 5640 V", "V = 0.012 × 470 = 5.64 V", "V = 0.012 ÷ 470 = 0.000 026 V"], "a": 2, "f": ["This divides R by I, which is the wrong rearrangement. V = IR means multiply, so the answer would be far too large.", "This uses 12 instead of 0.012. The mA conversion was ignored, so the answer is 1000 times too big.", "Right. Multiply the current in amps by the resistance: 0.012 × 470 = 5.64 V.", "This divides I by R. The equation V = IR multiplies them, so the answer would be tiny and wrong."]}, "trap": "Leaving the current in mA, or putting I on top when finding R. Convert mA to A first, then use $R = \\frac{V}{I}$ with V on top.", "recap": "Convert current to amps, then use $V = IR$ or $R = \\frac{V}{I}$, and give resistance in ohms (Ω)."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.1.2.1a", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 1 questions on Christian beliefs often ask you to explain a belief or to show that Christians hold different views.", "hook": {"q": "A person loves a child and has the power to stop the child being hurt. The child is hurt and the person does nothing. What would most people conclude?", "o": ["They have done nothing wrong, because loving people never step in", "Either they could not stop it, or they did not care enough to", "The child must be imagining it, as a loving person would act", "Love and power are unrelated, so nothing can be said about it"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Most people expect someone who loves a child and can help to step in. This assumes the opposite, and it misses the tension.", "Right. Love and power are both needed to stop the harm. If it happens anyway, one seems missing. This is the shape of the problem of evil.", "Wrong. This denies that the harm happened. The problem of evil starts from the fact that suffering is real.", "Wrong. Love and power are exactly what decide whether someone would help. The problem of evil depends on linking the two."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The problem of evil", "b": "Christians believe God is **omnipotent** (all-powerful) and **omnibenevolent** (all-loving). The **problem of evil** asks why such a God allows suffering. Many Christians reply that humans have free will, which explains moral evil, such as cruelty. Natural evil, such as earthquakes, is harder to explain."}, {"h": "The Trinity", "b": "Christians believe in **one God** who exists as three **persons**: Father, Son (Jesus) and Holy Spirit. The Father is linked with creating, the Son with saving, and the Spirit with God's power and presence today. The three are equal. They are not three separate gods."}, {"h": "Creation", "b": "**Genesis** 1 teaches that God created the universe over six days and called it good. Some Christians read this literally. Others read it as a symbolic story about God's purpose. John 1 says the **Word** (Jesus) was there at creation, and Genesis 1:2 mentions God's Spirit."}, {"h": "Life after death", "b": "Christians believe death is not the end, because Jesus rose from the dead. Many believe in **judgement**, then **heaven** or hell. Many Catholics believe in **purgatory**, where souls are made ready for heaven. Most Protestants reject this idea. Christians also differ on what hell is."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the problem of evil challenges some Christians' belief in God.", "steps": [{"do": "State the Christian belief about God: God is all-powerful and all-loving.", "why": "The problem only exists because of these two beliefs, so the answer must start there."}, {"do": "Link each quality to suffering: an all-loving God would want to stop it, and an all-powerful God would be able to.", "why": "This shows why these qualities seem to clash with suffering."}, {"do": "Give the evidence: suffering exists, for example an earthquake that kills thousands.", "why": "A concrete example shows the clash is real, not just theory."}, {"do": "Draw the conclusion: so God seems either not all-powerful or not all-loving, which challenges belief.", "why": "This answers 'why it challenges' directly and completes the chain of reasoning."}, {"do": "Add a Christian response: some reply that free will explains moral evil.", "why": "It shows awareness that Christians hold different views."}], "answer": "Christians believe God is all-powerful and all-loving. An all-loving God would want to stop suffering, and an all-powerful God could. Yet suffering exists, such as earthquakes that kill thousands. So either God is not all-powerful, or not all-loving, which challenges belief. Some Christians reply that free will explains moral evil."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention both 'all-powerful' and 'all-loving' together?", "o": ["Because Christians believe these are two separate gods", "Because every answer needs two key terms to earn marks", "Because the problem only arises if God has both qualities", "Because all-loving means God causes the suffering"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. Christians believe in one God. Treating qualities as separate gods is the same slip as thinking the Trinity means three gods.", "Wrong. Marks come from reasoning, not from counting terms. The terms are used because the argument depends on them.", "Right. If God lacked power or love, suffering would be less puzzling. The problem comes from God having both qualities.", "Wrong. All-loving means God cares for people. The problem is that a loving God seems to allow suffering, not cause it."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why many Christians believe in life after death.", "given": ["Point: Many Christians believe death is not the end and that God will judge people.", "Reason: They believe Jesus rose from the dead after the crucifixion."], "q": "Which is the best next step to develop the answer?", "o": ["Link: Christians believe in reincarnation, so people return to Earth in new bodies.", "Link: Protestants believe in purgatory, so everyone gets a second chance after death.", "Link: The Trinity means three gods, so there are three separate afterlives.", "Link: Christians hope to share in Jesus' new life, so they look forward to heaven."], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong. Reincarnation belongs to other religions. Christians believe in judgement and heaven or hell, not return to Earth.", "Wrong. Most Protestants reject purgatory, and it is not a second chance for everyone. It is a Catholic idea about preparing for heaven.", "Wrong. The Trinity is one God in three persons, not three gods, and it does not mean three afterlives.", "Right. It links the resurrection back to the question: Christians hope to share in Jesus' new life, so it gives them hope of heaven."]}, "trap": "Saying the Trinity means three gods. Christians believe in one God who exists as three persons. Also do not mix up moral evil (human choices) with natural evil (earthquakes): free will mainly explains the first.", "recap": "Christians believe in one God in three persons, all-loving and all-powerful, which makes suffering a problem; they also believe in judgement after death."}
{"s": "rs", "t": "3.1.5.1a", "kind": "writing", "why": "In 4-mark 'explain two' questions on Muslim beliefs, each point must be developed, not just stated, to earn both of its marks.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "How the 4 marks work", "b": "'Explain two ways…' gives 2 marks for each way. You earn one mark for a clear **point**. You earn the second mark when you **develop** it. To develop, say how or why, using words like 'so' or 'which means'."}, {"h": "Choose beliefs you know", "b": "Sunni Muslims hold six articles of faith, including **Tawhid**, angels (malaikah), al-Qadr and akhirah (life after death). Shi'a Muslims hold five roots: Tawhid, **Adalat** (justice), Nubuwwah (prophethood), **Imamah** (leadership by Imams) and Mi'ad (life after death). Pick the belief the question names."}, {"h": "Keep your two points apart", "b": "Your two points must be **different**. Write 'Muslims believe…' so you state beliefs as beliefs held by followers. Use the right terms, such as Allah, Jannah and shirk. Then show the **effect** on how Muslims live or think."}], "example": {"task": "Explain two ways in which belief in life after death (akhirah) may affect the life of a Muslim today. [4 marks]", "steps": [{"do": "Belief in akhirah encourages Muslims to live by Allah's rules.", "why": "This is a clear first point. It names one effect of the belief."}, {"do": "They believe angels record their deeds and Allah will judge them, so they pray, give zakat and are honest, hoping for Jannah.", "why": "This develops the point. It explains why, using 'so', and gives real examples of actions."}, {"do": "It can also give comfort.", "why": "This is a second, different point. It is not a repeat of the first."}, {"do": "Muslims believe death is not the end, so a grieving family can trust that a good person will be rewarded and suffering is temporary.", "why": "This develops the second point. It shows how the belief affects feelings and hope."}], "answer": "Belief in akhirah encourages Muslims to live by Allah's rules. They believe angels record their deeds and Allah will judge them, so they pray, give zakat and are honest, hoping for Jannah. It can also give comfort. Muslims believe death is not the end, so a grieving family can trust that a good person will be rewarded."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer add 'so they pray, give zakat and are honest' after the angels sentence?", "o": ["It repeats the point so that the answer looks longer", "It lists Pillars of Islam, which earn marks whatever the question asks", "It develops the point by showing how the belief changes what Muslims do", "It proves the belief is true, which the question requires"], "a": 2, "f": ["Repeating earns nothing extra. This part adds new information: the actions Muslims take because of the belief.", "Zakat is a Pillar, but it only earns marks here because it is linked to the belief. Unlinked lists do not gain marks.", "Right. The 'so' links the belief to its effect on behaviour. That link is what turns a point into a developed point.", "The question asks for effects on Muslims, not proof. RS answers state beliefs as beliefs held by followers; they do not prove them."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain two reasons why Tawhid is important to Muslims. [4 marks]", "given": ["Point 1: Tawhid is the most basic Muslim belief: Muslims believe Allah is one, with no partners.", "Development 1: The Shahadah, said to become a Muslim, begins 'There is no god but Allah'."], "q": "Which sentence should come next to start a different second reason?", "o": ["Tawhid is important because it is the foundation of Islam, as the Shahadah shows.", "Tawhid is also important because Muslims must worship Allah alone and avoid shirk.", "Tawhid is important because Muslims believe Allah is one and has no partners.", "Tawhid is also important because it teaches that Allah sends angels such as Jibril."], "a": 1, "f": ["This repeats reason 1. Being the foundation and the Shahadah were already used, so it earns no new marks.", "Right. This is a new reason: it affects worship. 'Avoid shirk' leaves room to develop it, for example by saying Muslims pray only to Allah.", "This only restates what Tawhid means. It gives no reason why it matters, and it duplicates the first point.", "Angels are a separate belief, not part of Tawhid. It does not answer why Tawhid is important."]}, "trap": "Writing two points that are really the same, or stating a belief with no development. After each point, add 'so' or 'which means' and say what Muslims do, think or feel because of it.", "recap": "For each 'explain two' answer, give a clear Muslim belief, then develop it with 'so' to show its effect on Muslims."}
